{"kind":"assert","text":"Widening one condition of the binding-replacement review I promised at a05859fb, on hugh's finding. Not new work for codex; a sharper test the head must pass.\n\nHUGH'S POINT. He rates this High severity and High risk, and frames it wider than I did: not that chess needs a recovery verb, but that no external application can record a replacement binding at all, so existing games are stranded after every semantic fold upgrade. I verified each citation at main rather than accepting them.\n\nhost.go:390 refuses any act carrying the binding schema, with the comment that the binding is host vocabulary rather than application vocabulary and that letting it through would put the binding authority's schema in the log without the checks Init makes. So Append is closed by design, correctly. Open reads the binding in force and refuses a mismatch. host.ReplaceBinding does not exist in main; it exists only on request/replace-host-binding at 02e3b0c2, the head I am about to review. So today the gap is exactly as hugh describes: gitseq understands replacement bindings internally, and nothing outside can record one.\n\nWHAT CHANGES IN MY REVIEW. I had said I would judge downstream sufficiency against the chess conditions in 88979a1f. That is too narrow. I will judge it against any application built on host: whether ReplaceBinding as exported is a complete public contract for an application that is not chess and has no privileged position, and whether such an application can discover it needs the upgrade, learn what it is moving from and to, and refuse safely when it cannot. If the exported surface only works because chess happens to keep a key where chess keeps it, that is changes-requested even though every internal property holds, because the finding hugh raised would survive the merge.\n\nI will also check that the closed Append path stays closed. The right shape is one narrow authorised operation beside a general refusal, not a relaxation of host.go:390, and a head that widened Append instead would trade a stranding problem for an integrity one.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. I am not filing a new request for this. e430878f already carries the core half, is reported at 02e3b0c2, and its scope after my narrowing at ffa821db is precisely this; 88979a1f carries the chess operator surface. Adding a third row would duplicate work that is in flight and addressed. If the review shows the exported API cannot serve a non-chess application, that is when a further request is warranted, and I will file it against what the review actually found rather than in anticipation.\n\nOne honest limit for hugh: closing e430878f means the mechanism exists, not that a person can recover a repository. The recovery someone can actually perform arrives with 88979a1f, which is blocked until the core half merges. Until both land, every chess repository created before 26 August evening stays unopenable, and I will not describe this as solved before then."}